# AMD driver software packaging
 
## Overview
 
[RFC0012](./RFC0012-Repo-Structure.md) reserves a top-level folder on
every stream subdomain of `repo.amd.com`, sibling to `rocm/`, for AMD
driver software. RFC0012 fixes *where* it sits and which stream
contract it inherits; this RFC defines *what goes in it and how it is
laid out*. Same split RFC0012 uses with
[RFC0021](./RFC0021-Repository-Package.md) for the tier packages.
 
The folder is named **`drivers/`** — RFC0012 reserved it as `amdgpu/`;
see *Naming*.
 
| Family | Contents | This RFC |
| :----- | :------- | :------- |
| Compute driver | `amdgpu` kernel (DKMS) driver | full |
| Virtualization | MXGPU SR-IOV host driver (`gim-dkms`) | full |
| Operator family | GPU Operator, device plugin, metrics exporter, node labeller | full |
| Graphics stack | display, OpenGL, Vulkan-Pro user-mode | placement only |
 
These share a namespace because they are all **driver lifecycle**
concerns, versioned against the driver rather than the ROCm Core SDK
major. The namespace is additionally intended to hold NIC, fabric, and
related platform drivers — see *Planned scope*.
 
## Motivation
 
Three migrations and one new publication target converge on this
folder:
 
- **Compute driver** and **MXGPU** are migrating off `repo.radeon.com`,
  whose `<product>/<version>/<distro>/` layout puts the version in the
  path — see *Why flat*.
- **Graphics stack** is a sibling `graphics/` folder there today, on an
  independent cadence.
- **Operator family** publishes charts from `rocm.github.io/gpu-operator`
  and has no native-package or image home on `repo.amd.com` at all.
 
Settled individually these would produce four layouts in one namespace.
This RFC fixes the layout once.
 
### Why one namespace
 
Sibling top-level `graphics/` and `operator/` folders were rejected:
splitting driver lifecycle across namespaces makes one provisioning
operation span three folders with three version stories.
 
Publishing the operator family under `rocm/` was rejected: it would
become an Expansion in RFC0012 terms and inherit per-ROCm-major pinning
and the central Python index obligations, neither of which applies.
 
A product subdomain (`operator.repo.amd.com`) is unavailable — under
RFC0012 the subdomain *is* the stream selector and that set is closed.
 
### Naming
 
`amdgpu` is the kernel module name (`amdgpu.ko`, `amdgpu-dkms`). As a
namespace it under-describes the folder: the graphics stack is not the
`amdgpu` module, the GPU Operator is not an `amdgpu` artifact, and the
namespace is planned to hold NIC drivers, which are not GPUs.
 
The rename lands before any content is published under the reserved
path, while it is still free. `amdgpu` remains the kernel driver
package name.
 
### Planned scope: platform drivers
 
The namespace is scoped to **AMD driver software**, not GPU driver
software. NIC, fabric, and related platform drivers are expected here,
per the unified-delivery requirement that an administrator install GPU
driver, NIC driver, and ROCm through uniform package-manager commands
from one repository. Today only the GPU half is satisfiable; the naming
and layout are chosen so the rest is a content change, not a structural
one.
 
Their package naming, pinning, partner-certification rules, and owning
organization are **not** settled here — separate RFC. This RFC commits
only to the namespace holding them.
 
## Scope
 
**In scope:** layout, artifact classes, and migration for the compute
driver, MXGPU, and the operator family.
 
**Placement only — graphics stack.** This RFC places graphics in
`drivers/` and asserts it inherits the structural rules below. It does
**not** define graphics requirements; cadence, packaging, component
breakdown, version lines, and support matrix belong to the graphics
organization in a separate RFC. Read packaging specifics below as
applying to the compute driver.
 
**Not in scope:** ROCm Core SDK, expansions, extras, and third-party AI
forks (under `rocm/`, per RFC0012); `amdgpu-install` (being retired —
the `Conflicts:` rule in RFC0021 stands); AMDVLK (frozen, archival);
AMF (no migration).
 
## Inherited from RFC0012
 
Not restated here: stream subdomains and the closed stream set,
identical tree across streams, no cross-stream pathing, per-stream
retention, repo setup via the tier packages in the singleton
`amdrepos/` folder, GPG keys from `amdrepos/gpg/`, build variant
separation, and the RFC0009 distro identifier set.
 
## Structure
 
`drivers/` and `rocm/` are parallel namespaces, each with its own
`packages/` folder — two separate package repositories, not one
referenced twice:
 
```
<stream>.repo.amd.com/
  |
  +-- rocm/                  # ROCm Core SDK, expansions      (RFC0012)
  |     core/packages/       #   ROCm native packages
  |     whl/  whl-next/
  |
  +-- drivers/               # AMD driver software           (this RFC)
        packages/<distro>/   #   driver native packages (.deb / .rpm)
        charts/              #   Helm repo: index.yaml + archives
        containers/          #   image reference manifest (no binaries)
```
 
They never hold the same package. The separation is what lets a node
take a driver upgrade without touching ROCm, and the reverse.
 
Only `packages/` is a package-manager repository. `charts/` is a Helm
repository; `containers/` holds references only.
 
### Flat package repository
 
`drivers/packages/` is **flat**, matching the rule RFC0012 states for
`rocm/core/packages/`.
 
**"Flat" constrains the version axis only** — it does not mean no
subdirectories. Two axes:
 
| Axis | In the path? | Expressed as |
| :--- | :----------- | :----------- |
| Distro / OS version | **yes** | one subfolder per RFC0009 distro ID |
| Package version | **no** | package name + version metadata |
| Build variant | separable | sibling folder, distinct index, or disabled-by-default stanza |
 
```
<stream>.repo.amd.com/drivers/packages/
  ubuntu2204/   ubuntu2404/   debian12/   rhel8/
  rhel9/        rhel10/       sles15/     azl3/
```
 
Each distro folder is internally flat. A node configures exactly one
and never edits it again.
 
Normative:
 
- **No version in the path** — no `<version>/` or date-stamped
  subfolders inside a distro folder.
- **All retained versions co-resident** in each distro folder, so
  `dnf --showduplicates` / `apt-cache policy` list every one.
- **Retention is pruning** of old versions, not deletion of folders.
- **Upgrade and downgrade are package-manager operations only** — no
  repository reconfiguration, no structure change.
- **The distro folder is stable** for the life of that OS version's
  support.
- **Variant separation** — standard is the default; a package manager
  pointed at the standard repository never resolves a variant
  accidentally.
 
RFC0012 permits realizing the distro axis as a `deb/` + `rpm/` grouping
carrying the OS profile in the index instead. Per-distro folders are
the expected realization and what the examples assume; what is
normative is that a node resolves exactly one distro profile, stable
across versions.
 
#### Why flat
 
On `repo.radeon.com` both axes are in the path, so each version is a
different repository:
 
```
repo.radeon.com/amdgpu/30.10/ubuntu/   <-- configured repo
repo.radeon.com/amdgpu/30.20/ubuntu/   <-- a DIFFERENT repo
                       ^^^^^  ^^^^^^
                       version  distro
```
 
Moving a fleet between versions means rewriting every node's sources
file; rolling back means rewriting it again. Here only the distro axis
is in the path:
 
```
# configured once at provisioning
https://stable.repo.amd.com/drivers/packages/ubuntu2404/
 
$ dnf --showduplicates list amdgpu-dkms
$ dnf install amdgpu-dkms-30.20      # upgrade
$ dnf downgrade amdgpu-dkms-30.10    # and back
```
 
### Version-line reconciliation
 
The compute driver carries two concurrent version lines on
`repo.radeon.com` (ROCm-aligned and Radeon Software for Linux). A flat
tree cannot host two unrelated sequences under one package name without
ordering ambiguity.
 
**Requirement:** one monotonic sequence per package name. Where both
lines stay visible they become distinct package names. Naming is left
to the implementation; the ambiguity must be resolved before cutover.
 
`latest/` has no equivalent — in a flat repository "latest" is what the
package manager resolves by default.
 
### MXGPU
 
Publishes into `drivers/packages/<distro>/` under the compute driver's
rules. Three differences:
 
- **Host side only** — ships `gim-dkms` (GPU-IOV Module), which runs on
  the hypervisor. Guests run the ordinary GPU driver already in this
  namespace, so MXGPU adds a package, not a guest tree.
- **Third version line** (`9.3.0.K` as of 2026-10), independent of both
  driver lines. Distinct package name, so the rule above is already
  satisfied.
- **Distro identifier conversion** — the existing tree keys Debian by
  codename (`jammy/`, `noble/`) and carries both `el9/` and `rhel9/`.
  Migration maps these to RFC0009 identifiers and collapses the
  duplication.
 
### Helm charts and container images
 
RFC0012 defines no artifact class for Helm charts or OCI images; the
operator family ships both, so they are defined here.
 
**`drivers/charts/`** — a Helm chart repository per stream, added like
any other:
 
```
$ helm repo add amd-drivers https://stable.repo.amd.com/drivers/charts/
$ helm install gpu-operator amd-drivers/gpu-operator-charts
```
 
Canonical location for GPU Operator charts; `rocm.github.io/gpu-operator`
migrates here behind a redirect. Chart versions use the chart's own
`version` / `appVersion` and are not required to track the ROCm or
driver schemes. Each chart declares its supported driver and Kubernetes
ranges in metadata.
 
**`drivers/containers/`** — the image reference manifest per stream,
listing the fully qualified names and tags that the same stream's
charts resolve to, so an image set is inspectable and pinnable without
installing a chart. Images live in an OCI registry, not in the tree.
 
- Backing registry is an implementation decision; images must have a
  lifecycle matching the stream's retention window.
- **Stream mapping is by tag, not by registry.** One registry serves
  every stream, preserving RFC0012's rule that a stream subdomain
  serves only its own artifacts.
 
## Stream coverage
 
| Family | Streams |
| :----- | :------ |
| Compute driver | all streams RFC0012 defines |
| Operator family | `nightly`, `weekly`, `rc`, `stable` — **not `dev`** |
| MXGPU | `rc`, `stable` |
| Graphics stack | out of scope (graphics RFC) |
 
The operator family skips `dev` because per-commit churn has no
consumer for a cluster-deployed component and the image build cost is
prohibitive. MXGPU cadence is set by the virtualization organization
and does not track ROCm promotion gates. `ltsrc` / `lts` deferred with
the rest of the LTS work.
 
## Relationship to ROCm
 
Driver, operator, and ROCm Core SDK version independently — a node may
run a driver and operator from `stable` while containers scheduled onto
it carry any supported ROCm version. Compatibility is expressed as
declared ranges in package and chart metadata, not as a shared version
number and not by folder co-location.
 
## Migration
 
1. `drivers/` opens on `stable` and `rc`, per RFC0012's v1 set.
2. Compute driver packages migrate from `repo.radeon.com`. Legacy paths
   are hard-deprecated — frozen, redirected, nothing deleted.
3. MXGPU migrates from `repo.radeon.com/mxgpu/`, same treatment, with
   codenames converted to RFC0009 identifiers. Operator charts migrate
   from `rocm.github.io/gpu-operator` into `drivers/charts/`.
4. Remaining stream subdomains follow RFC0012's v2 set.
5. Historical `repo.radeon.com` content lands under
   `archives.repo.amd.com`, per RFC0012.
 
Graphics stack migration follows the same pattern; timing and
sequencing are set by the graphics organization's RFC.
 
## Open questions
 
- **NIC and fabric packaging rules** — namespace is committed to
  holding them; naming, pinning, partner certification, and ownership
  need their own RFC and cross-org sign-off.
- **Registry selection** for operator images, and whether existing
  image references are retagged or only new builds adopt the scheme.
- **Compatibility-manifest ownership** — which group owns the declared
  driver / operator / ROCm ranges, given overlap with the system BOM
  compatibility data.
- **Graphics requirements RFC** — cadence, version lines, and whether
  graphics adopts the stream model are unowned by this document.
